WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:06.916
This is the rapid-fire track: every answer in this episode, back to back, one breath each.

2
00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:11.443
No teaching, no traps, just the answers in the shape you would write them.

3
00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:15.297
It is made for the night before, and for the bus on the way in.

4
00:00:15.297 --> 00:00:18.967
If one of these sounds unfamiliar, go back to its own track.

5
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:22.081
<v Examiner>Case L, Jack's hearing loss.

6
00:00:22.531 --> 00:00:26.099
The hair cells of the organ of Corti, on the basilar membrane,

7
00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.940
in the cochlea of the inner ear.

8
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.773
Loud, high-amplitude sound bends the hairs against the tectorial membrane until they

9
00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:36.915
break off: eighty-five decibels over time, a hundred and thirty at once.

10
00:00:36.915 --> 00:00:41.345
No impulses go down nerve eight to the brain, and hair cells don't grow back,

11
00:00:41.345 --> 00:00:42.381
so it's permanent.

12
00:00:42.381 --> 00:00:44.107
That's sensorineural deafness.

13
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:48.077
The other kind, conduction, is a blockage in the outer or middle ear,

14
00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:51.011
like earwax or glue ear, and it's often reversible.

15
00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:53.947
<v Examiner>Case L, otosclerosis.

16
00:00:54.397 --> 00:00:55.593
Conduction deafness.

17
00:00:55.593 --> 00:00:59.003
The ossicles, the malleus, incus and stapes, are damaged.

18
00:00:59.003 --> 00:01:03.969
They sit in the middle ear and carry vibration from the eardrum to the oval window.

19
00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:08.037
In otosclerosis they become attached to the walls of the middle ear,

20
00:01:08.037 --> 00:01:13.063
so they can't vibrate or amplify the sound, and it isn't conducted to the inner ear.

21
00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:15.576
The inner ear and its hair cells are fine,

22
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:18.866
which is the difference from Jack's sensorineural loss.

23
00:01:20.116 --> 00:01:22.377
<v Examiner>Case 11, Pania's milk coming in.

24
00:01:22.827 --> 00:01:28.299
Prolactin, from the anterior pituitary, stimulates the alveolar cells to produce milk.

25
00:01:28.299 --> 00:01:33.198
Oxytocin, made in the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary,

26
00:01:33.198 --> 00:01:36.570
makes the myoepithelial cells contract: the let-down.

27
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.115
Both are triggered by the baby suckling.

28
00:01:39.115 --> 00:01:41.914
The mammary gland is a modified sweat gland:

29
00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:47.640
fifteen to twenty-five lobes around the nipple, lobules of milk-making alveoli, an areola,

30
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.231
and suspensory ligaments.

31
00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:53.112
Milk goes alveoli, alveolar ducts, lactiferous ducts, nipple.

32
00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:58.774
In pregnancy, progesterone builds the lobules and alveoli, and oestrogen grows the ducts.

33
00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:01.901
<v Examiner>Case 11, the hormone table.

34
00:02:02.351 --> 00:02:06.138
Ducts and glands grow: oestrogen and progesterone.

35
00:02:06.138 --> 00:02:08.941
Blood flow to the breasts: oestrogen.

36
00:02:08.941 --> 00:02:11.516
Lobules and alveoli: progesterone.

37
00:02:11.516 --> 00:02:13.561
Milk production: prolactin.

38
00:02:13.561 --> 00:02:18.181
Milk ejection, the myoepithelial cells contracting: oxytocin.

39
00:02:18.181 --> 00:02:20.378
Pipes, factories, fill, taps.

40
00:02:21.628 --> 00:02:22.737
<v Examiner>Pedigree charts.

41
00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:26.775
Squares are males, circles are females, shaded is affected.

42
00:02:26.775 --> 00:02:29.572
In every generation, never skipping: dominant.

43
00:02:29.572 --> 00:02:31.397
Skips a generation: recessive.

44
00:02:31.397 --> 00:02:36.201
Males and females about equal: autosomal, and father to son rules out X-linked.

45
00:02:36.201 --> 00:02:38.512
Label the homozygous recessives first,

46
00:02:38.512 --> 00:02:42.160
then work out everyone else from their parents and children:

47
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.721
the affected father is big B little b, and the mother is little b little b.

48
00:02:46.721 --> 00:02:50.917
The Punnett square gives two big B little b to two little b little b:

49
00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:53.289
a fifty per cent chance for each child.

50
00:02:54.539 --> 00:02:55.840
<v Examiner>Huntington's disease.

51
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.162
A mutated gene on chromosome four, an autosome:

52
00:02:59.162 --> 00:03:04.478
C A G repeated thirty-six to a hundred and twenty times, instead of ten to thirty-five.

53
00:03:04.478 --> 00:03:09.734
It makes an abnormal, toxic huntingtin protein that builds up in the brain and damages

54
00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:11.567
movement, thinking and memory.

55
00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:16.150
It's autosomal dominant: one copy is enough, there are no healthy carriers,

56
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.167
and it shows in every generation.

57
00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:21.039
Big H little h, crossed with little h little h,

58
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:23.789
gives a fifty per cent chance for each child.

59
00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:26.468
<v Examiner>Glaucoma and cataracts.

60
00:03:26.918 --> 00:03:32.315
Glaucoma: aqueous humour is made by the ciliary body and drained by the canal of Schlemm.

61
00:03:32.315 --> 00:03:36.500
If the drain blocks, fluid builds up, the intraocular pressure rises,

62
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.987
and it damages the retina and the optic nerve, which can end in blindness.

63
00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:44.929
A cataract: the proteins of the lens denature, so it goes cloudy.

64
00:03:44.929 --> 00:03:47.840
Vision becomes blurred and yellowed, with glare.

65
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.811
It's treated with surgery that replaces the lens.

66
00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:53.341
<v Examiner>The words of genetics.

67
00:03:53.791 --> 00:03:56.317
Homozygous: the alleles are the same.

68
00:03:56.317 --> 00:03:58.501
Heterozygous: they're different.

69
00:03:58.501 --> 00:04:00.412
Allele: a version of a gene.

70
00:04:00.412 --> 00:04:03.279
Dominant: always appears in the phenotype.

71
00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:06.964
Recessive: appears only if no other allele is present.

72
00:04:06.964 --> 00:04:09.148
Genotype: the alleles inherited.

73
00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.469
Phenotype: the traits you can see.

74
00:04:11.469 --> 00:04:15.496
Autosomal: a disease gene on chromosomes one to twenty-two.

75
00:04:15.496 --> 00:04:18.568
X-linked: on the X, and more common in males.

76
00:04:18.568 --> 00:04:21.366
Locus: where a gene sits on a chromosome.

77
00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:24.983
Chromosomes: lengths of DNA carrying genes, in pairs.

78
00:04:24.983 --> 00:04:27.372
Genome: all the DNA in the nucleus.

79
00:04:27.372 --> 00:04:30.785
Autosome: a chromosome numbered one to twenty-two.

80
00:04:30.785 --> 00:04:32.901
Sex chromosome: the X or the Y.

81
00:04:32.901 --> 00:04:36.382
Variation: differences in genes across populations.

82
00:04:36.382 --> 00:04:39.999
Inheritance: how genes pass from parents to children.

83
00:04:41.249 --> 00:04:42.252
<v Examiner>The Punnett square.

84
00:04:42.702 --> 00:04:45.473
Alleles come in pairs, one from each parent,

85
00:04:45.473 --> 00:04:50.764
and the Punnett square shows every possible combination, so the probability of each.

86
00:04:50.764 --> 00:04:53.976
One parent across the top, the other down the side.

87
00:04:53.976 --> 00:04:58.197
Big B little b crossed with big B little b gives one to two to one:

88
00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:02.039
twenty-five per cent homozygous dominant, fifty heterozygous,

89
00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:04.117
twenty-five homozygous recessive.

90
00:05:04.117 --> 00:05:07.204
So seventy-five per cent brown, twenty-five blue.

91
00:05:07.204 --> 00:05:10.164
In codominance, I A and I B are both expressed:

92
00:05:10.164 --> 00:05:15.518
I A little i crossed with I B little i gives group A B, group A, group B and group O,

93
00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:17.155
twenty-five per cent each.

94
00:05:18.405 --> 00:05:19.749
<v Examiner>Patterns of inheritance.

95
00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:23.856
Codominance: both alleles are fully expressed, like A B blood,

96
00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.334
where I A and I B together give group A B.

97
00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:29.991
Incomplete dominance: one allele doesn't fully mask the other,

98
00:05:29.991 --> 00:05:31.996
so the heterozygote is in between,

99
00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:35.535
like sickle cell carriers with both normal and sickle cells.

100
00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:39.487
Polygenic: many genes control one trait, giving a continuous range,

101
00:05:39.487 --> 00:05:41.138
like height and skin colour.

102
00:05:41.138 --> 00:05:45.916
Mitochondrial: mitochondrial DNA comes only from the mother, to all her children,

103
00:05:45.916 --> 00:05:49.042
and severity varies because of the bottleneck effect.

104
00:05:50.292 --> 00:05:52.553
<v Examiner>Multiple alleles and X-linked genes.

105
00:05:53.003 --> 00:05:58.314
Multiple alleles: one trait with more than two alleles, like ABO blood: three alleles,

106
00:05:58.314 --> 00:05:59.981
six genotypes, four groups.

107
00:05:59.981 --> 00:06:03.687
The A and B alleles are codominant and both dominant over O,

108
00:06:03.687 --> 00:06:08.009
so AO crossed with BO gives A, B, AB and O, twenty-five per cent each.

109
00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:09.182
It isn't polygenic.

110
00:06:09.182 --> 00:06:13.135
X-linked: the gene is on the X and the Y has no matching allele,

111
00:06:13.135 --> 00:06:18.507
so one copy shows in a male while a female needs two, and only females can be carriers.

112
00:06:18.507 --> 00:06:23.386
A carrier mother and a normal father: half the sons affected, and no daughters.

113
00:06:23.386 --> 00:06:28.758
An affected father and a normal mother: no affected sons, and every daughter a carrier.

114
00:06:30.008 --> 00:06:32.269
<v Examiner>DNA, chromosomes and mutations.

115
00:06:32.719 --> 00:06:36.863
DNA, deoxyribonucleic acid, is a double helix of nucleotides,

116
00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:40.599
with adenine paired to thymine and guanine to cytosine.

117
00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:43.859
It stores the genetic code and passes traits on.

118
00:06:43.859 --> 00:06:49.972
RNA is single-stranded, with ribose and uracil, and carries the code out to make proteins.

119
00:06:49.972 --> 00:06:52.893
A chromosome is coiled DNA with many genes,

120
00:06:52.893 --> 00:06:56.968
and a gene is a segment that codes for a trait or a protein.

121
00:06:56.968 --> 00:07:02.810
A gamete has twenty-three chromosomes and the zygote forty-six, half from each parent.

122
00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:06.478
A mutation is a permanent change in a gene's DNA code.

123
00:07:06.478 --> 00:07:10.689
A genetic disease comes from a faulty gene, like Huntington's,

124
00:07:10.689 --> 00:07:12.659
cystic fibrosis or Tay-Sachs.

125
00:07:12.659 --> 00:07:15.919
Aneuploidy is an abnormal number of chromosomes.

126
00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:18.876
<v Examiner>Karyotypes and aneuploidy.

127
00:07:19.326 --> 00:07:24.106
A karyotype is a picture of the chromosomes in numbered homologous pairs:

128
00:07:24.106 --> 00:07:29.934
forty-six chromosomes, twenty-three pairs, twenty-two pairs of autosomes plus X X or X Y.

129
00:07:29.934 --> 00:07:33.077
Aneuploidy is an abnormal number of chromosomes.

130
00:07:33.077 --> 00:07:37.071
Down syndrome is trisomy twenty-one, forty-seven chromosomes.

131
00:07:37.071 --> 00:07:40.149
Turner syndrome is forty-five, with only one X.

132
00:07:40.149 --> 00:07:44.602
It arises from non-disjunction in meiosis: a pair fails to separate,

133
00:07:44.602 --> 00:07:47.614
so a gamete gets one too many, or one too few.

134
00:07:48.864 --> 00:07:51.104
<v Examiner>Detecting and treating genetic disease.

135
00:07:51.554 --> 00:07:56.797
Genetic testing reads a person's DNA, often from a blood sample: a karyotype,

136
00:07:56.797 --> 00:07:59.521
or a CGH array for missing or extra DNA.

137
00:07:59.521 --> 00:08:04.014
Chorionic villus sampling takes placenta at eight to twelve weeks.

138
00:08:04.014 --> 00:08:08.304
Amniocentesis takes amniotic fluid at fourteen to twenty weeks.

139
00:08:08.304 --> 00:08:10.143
Both check the fetal cells.

140
00:08:10.143 --> 00:08:13.956
Genetic engineering is the deliberate alteration of DNA,

141
00:08:13.956 --> 00:08:16.543
like bacteria altered to make insulin.

142
00:08:16.543 --> 00:08:22.399
Gene therapy treats a genetic disease by inserting genetic material into the patient's

143
00:08:22.399 --> 00:08:22.807
cells.

144
00:08:24.057 --> 00:08:25.572
<v Examiner>The genetics fine print.

145
00:08:26.022 --> 00:08:28.258
A line through a symbol: deceased.

146
00:08:28.258 --> 00:08:30.099
A horizontal line: a couple.

147
00:08:30.099 --> 00:08:33.124
Their children hang from one line as siblings.

148
00:08:33.124 --> 00:08:37.201
Generations are Roman numerals, people numbered left to right.

149
00:08:37.201 --> 00:08:41.147
Y-linked: only males, every generation, father to every son.

150
00:08:41.147 --> 00:08:44.566
Victoria was a carrier, and Leopold had haemophilia.

151
00:08:44.566 --> 00:08:50.090
A trait is a characteristic determined by genes, and a mutagen changes the DNA code.

152
00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:55.943
Haploid is twenty-three, diploid forty-six, and pair twenty-three is the sex chromosomes.

153
00:08:55.943 --> 00:09:00.217
Mitochondrial DNA is part of the genome, but not the nuclear DNA.

154
00:09:00.217 --> 00:09:04.294
Her key: most human traits are determined by multiple alleles.

155
00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:06.813
A one-trait cross is monohybrid.

156
00:09:06.813 --> 00:09:10.240
Outside the Punnett square, gametes; inside, zygotes.

157
00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.408
A test cross uses a homozygous recessive partner,

158
00:09:13.408 --> 00:09:17.417
and one recessive offspring proves the parent is heterozygous.

159
00:09:17.417 --> 00:09:22.977
Red and white giving pink is incomplete dominance; red and white parts is codominance.

160
00:09:22.977 --> 00:09:28.085
Two sickle cell carriers have a twenty-five per cent chance of the lethal form.

161
00:09:28.085 --> 00:09:31.188
Polygenic: many genes, each with a small effect.

162
00:09:31.188 --> 00:09:34.615
Two non-freckled parents can't have a freckled child.

163
00:09:34.615 --> 00:09:37.460
The most common fetal test is amniocentesis;

164
00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.499
her most reliable is chorionic villus sampling.

165
00:09:41.749 --> 00:09:43.285
<v Examiner>The three regions of the ear.

166
00:09:43.735 --> 00:09:47.861
The outer ear: the pinna, the external auditory canal with its cerumen,

167
00:09:47.861 --> 00:09:49.372
and the tympanic membrane.

168
00:09:49.372 --> 00:09:52.569
It collects sound onto the eardrum and helps locate it.

169
00:09:52.569 --> 00:09:56.521
The middle ear: air-filled, in the temporal bone, with the ossicles,

170
00:09:56.521 --> 00:09:58.904
which amplify sound onto the oval window.

171
00:09:58.904 --> 00:10:03.612
The Eustachian tube equalises pressure, and two small muscles dampen loud sounds.

172
00:10:03.612 --> 00:10:08.727
The inner ear: a bony labyrinth of perilymph around a membranous labyrinth of endolymph.

173
00:10:08.727 --> 00:10:11.284
The cochlea, with its organ of Corti, hears.

174
00:10:11.284 --> 00:10:13.260
The vestibular apparatus balances.

175
00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:15.705
<v Examiner>How hearing happens.

176
00:10:16.155 --> 00:10:20.688
The pinna collects sound down the canal, and the tympanic membrane vibrates.

177
00:10:20.688 --> 00:10:25.043
The ossicles, malleus, incus and stapes, amplify it onto the oval window.

178
00:10:25.043 --> 00:10:29.636
That moves the perilymph, then the endolymph, and the basilar membrane moves,

179
00:10:29.636 --> 00:10:34.169
so the hair cells of the organ of Corti bend against the tectorial membrane.

180
00:10:34.169 --> 00:10:36.555
That's transduction into nerve impulses,

181
00:10:36.555 --> 00:10:41.029
which travel along nerve eight to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.

182
00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:44.221
<v Examiner>Static and dynamic equilibrium.

183
00:10:44.671 --> 00:10:49.152
Both are sensed by hair cells in the vestibular apparatus of the inner ear,

184
00:10:49.152 --> 00:10:52.737
and sent along nerve eight to the brain stem and cerebellum.

185
00:10:52.737 --> 00:10:57.756
Static equilibrium is head position relative to gravity, and straight-line movement.

186
00:10:57.756 --> 00:11:01.102
It's detected by the maculae in the utricle and saccule,

187
00:11:01.102 --> 00:11:03.552
where otoliths slide when the head tilts.

188
00:11:03.552 --> 00:11:06.360
Dynamic equilibrium is balance during rotation.

189
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.304
It's detected by the crista ampullaris in each semicircular canal,

190
00:11:10.304 --> 00:11:13.769
where the endolymph swings the cupula and bends the hairs.

191
00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:16.406
<v Examiner>The three layers of the eye.

192
00:11:16.856 --> 00:11:21.305
The fibrous tunic: the sclera, which protects the eye and anchors its muscles,

193
00:11:21.305 --> 00:11:25.126
and the cornea, which does about eighty per cent of the refraction.

194
00:11:25.126 --> 00:11:29.689
The vascular tunic: the choroid, rich in blood vessels, which nourishes the eye;

195
00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:34.481
the ciliary body, which holds the lens, changes its shape, and makes aqueous humour;

196
00:11:34.481 --> 00:11:36.762
and the iris, which controls pupil size.

197
00:11:36.762 --> 00:11:41.781
The neural tunic, the retina: a pigmented layer that absorbs light, then photoreceptors,

198
00:11:41.781 --> 00:11:46.915
rods and cones, then bipolar cells, then ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve.

199
00:11:48.165 --> 00:11:49.999
<v Examiner>The retina, and colour blindness.

200
00:11:50.449 --> 00:11:53.800
The photoreceptors are rods and cones, in the retina.

201
00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:59.047
Cones sit in the macula lutea, packed in the fovea centralis: bright light, colour,

202
00:11:59.047 --> 00:12:00.564
and the sharpest vision.

203
00:12:00.564 --> 00:12:06.254
Rods are absent from the fovea and increase towards the edges: dim light, black and white,

204
00:12:06.254 --> 00:12:08.656
and many rods share one ganglion cell.

205
00:12:08.656 --> 00:12:13.018
Pigments differ by their opsin, and the optic disc is the blind spot.

206
00:12:13.018 --> 00:12:15.231
Colour blindness affects the cones,

207
00:12:15.231 --> 00:12:20.351
found mostly in the macula lutea and the fovea centralis: in red-green blindness,

208
00:12:20.351 --> 00:12:21.489
the L and M cones.

209
00:12:22.739 --> 00:12:23.635
<v Examiner>Near vision.

210
00:12:24.085 --> 00:12:29.397
The ciliary muscle contracts, so the lens bulges and gets rounder: accommodation.

211
00:12:29.397 --> 00:12:30.774
The pupils constrict.

212
00:12:30.774 --> 00:12:33.069
The eyes converge, turning inwards.

213
00:12:33.069 --> 00:12:38.447
For distant vision, all three undo: the ciliary muscle relaxes, the lens flattens,

214
00:12:38.447 --> 00:12:39.890
and the pupils dilate.

215
00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:44.480
Near light arrives diverging; from over six metres away it's parallel.

216
00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.077
<v Examiner>Myopia, hyperopia and astigmatism.

217
00:12:48.527 --> 00:12:51.172
Emmetropia: light is focused on the retina.

218
00:12:51.172 --> 00:12:56.525
Myopia, short-sighted: distant light focuses in front of the retina because the eyeball

219
00:12:56.525 --> 00:12:59.663
is too long, and a concave lens diverges the light.

220
00:12:59.663 --> 00:13:01.140
Hyperopia, long-sighted:

221
00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:05.447
near light focuses behind the retina because the eyeball is too short,

222
00:13:05.447 --> 00:13:07.785
and a convex lens converges the light.

223
00:13:07.785 --> 00:13:10.923
Astigmatism: the cornea or lens is curved unevenly,

224
00:13:10.923 --> 00:13:14.677
so light focuses at several points and everything is blurred.

225
00:13:14.677 --> 00:13:16.892
Glasses, contacts, or laser surgery.

226
00:13:18.142 --> 00:13:19.571
<v Examiner>Waves of light and sound.

227
00:13:20.021 --> 00:13:24.220
Amplitude is the wave's height: loudness in decibels, or brightness.

228
00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.184
Wavelength is peak to peak: the colour of light.

229
00:13:27.184 --> 00:13:30.889
Frequency is waves per second, in hertz: the pitch of sound,

230
00:13:30.889 --> 00:13:33.977
and a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength.

231
00:13:33.977 --> 00:13:36.756
Light is electromagnetic and needs no medium;

232
00:13:36.756 --> 00:13:39.905
sound is a mechanical pressure wave that needs one.

233
00:13:39.905 --> 00:13:42.746
Reflection: light bounces back, like a mirror.

234
00:13:42.746 --> 00:13:47.377
Refraction: light bends as it slows in a different density, like a rainbow,

235
00:13:47.377 --> 00:13:49.724
or the cornea and lens focusing light.

236
00:13:49.724 --> 00:13:54.602
Sound reflects as an echo or sonar, and refracts, bending away from hot ground.

237
00:13:55.852 --> 00:13:56.492
<v Examiner>Taste.

238
00:13:56.942 --> 00:13:58.465
Taste is a chemical sense:

239
00:13:58.465 --> 00:14:02.212
taste buds are chemoreceptors for chemicals dissolved in saliva.

240
00:14:02.212 --> 00:14:05.549
They sit on the papillae, fungiform, vallate and foliate,

241
00:14:05.549 --> 00:14:09.589
and each bud is a cluster of gustatory cells in a supporting capsule,

242
00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:11.873
with gustatory hairs in the taste pore.

243
00:14:11.873 --> 00:14:14.801
Five tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami.

244
00:14:14.801 --> 00:14:19.660
The facial nerve carries the front two-thirds, the glossopharyngeal the back third,

245
00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:23.232
and the vagus the very back, to the brain stem, the thalamus,

246
00:14:23.232 --> 00:14:25.106
the insula and the frontal lobe.

247
00:14:25.106 --> 00:14:26.745
Flavour is taste plus smell.

248
00:14:27.995 --> 00:14:28.614
<v Examiner>Smell.

249
00:14:29.064 --> 00:14:34.184
The olfactory epithelium is in the roof of the nasal cavity, which is why you sniff.

250
00:14:34.184 --> 00:14:38.024
It has olfactory receptor neurons with cilia, supporting cells,

251
00:14:38.024 --> 00:14:40.523
and basal cells that replace the neurons.

252
00:14:40.523 --> 00:14:43.875
Odourants dissolve in the mucus and bind the receptors.

253
00:14:43.875 --> 00:14:47.837
The axons pass through the cribriform plate as cranial nerve one,

254
00:14:47.837 --> 00:14:51.372
and synapse in the olfactory bulb, under the frontal lobe.

255
00:14:51.372 --> 00:14:55.700
The primary olfactory cortex in the temporal lobe identifies the smell;

256
00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.991
the amygdala adds emotion, and the hippocampus memory.

257
00:14:58.991 --> 00:15:01.917
Total loss is anosmia; partial loss is hyposmia.

258
00:15:03.167 --> 00:15:04.575
<v Examiner>The eye, part by part.

259
00:15:05.025 --> 00:15:08.856
The conjunctiva covers the front of the eye and lines the eyelids,

260
00:15:08.856 --> 00:15:10.539
and it's infected in pinkeye.

261
00:15:10.539 --> 00:15:13.847
The cornea is the clear window where light is first bent:

262
00:15:13.847 --> 00:15:17.446
eighty per cent of the refraction, and it can be transplanted.

263
00:15:17.446 --> 00:15:22.438
The aqueous humour fills the anterior chamber and drains through the canal of Schlemm.

264
00:15:22.438 --> 00:15:27.023
The iris has circular and radial muscle, and blue eyes have less brown pigment.

265
00:15:27.023 --> 00:15:30.157
The ciliary body holds the lens, makes aqueous humour,

266
00:15:30.157 --> 00:15:33.059
and its involuntary muscles change the lens shape.

267
00:15:33.059 --> 00:15:36.309
The lens is biconvex: twenty per cent of the refraction.

268
00:15:36.309 --> 00:15:41.185
The vitreous humour is the jelly behind the lens, with the hyaloid canal through it.

269
00:15:41.635 --> 00:15:45.030
The retina is the sensory layer, where the image forms.

270
00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:49.660
The macula lutea is the yellow spot, and the fovea, in line with the pupil,

271
00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.586
has only cones.

272
00:15:50.586 --> 00:15:55.278
The optic disc, the blind spot, is where the axons leave as the optic nerve,

273
00:15:55.278 --> 00:15:56.636
to the occipital lobe.

274
00:15:56.636 --> 00:16:00.525
The choroid is black, to stop reflection, and feeds the retina.

275
00:16:00.525 --> 00:16:03.920
The sclera is tough and white, and keeps the eye round.

276
00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:06.390
Vision has most of the body's receptors.

277
00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.427
Strabismus: the eyes can't align.

278
00:16:08.427 --> 00:16:10.896
Myopia: too bulgy a lens, biconcave fix.

279
00:16:10.896 --> 00:16:12.995
Hyperopia: too flat, biconvex fix.

280
00:16:14.245 --> 00:16:15.952
<v Examiner>The ear and sound, fine print.

281
00:16:16.402 --> 00:16:18.934
The pinna is cartilage, with the ear lobe,

282
00:16:18.934 --> 00:16:22.130
and channels sound down the external auditory meatus,

283
00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.231
where ceruminous glands make wax that can cause conduction deafness.

284
00:16:26.231 --> 00:16:31.416
The eardrum turns air vibrations into bone vibrations, and the ossicles act as levers;

285
00:16:31.416 --> 00:16:34.069
the stapes is the smallest bone in the body.

286
00:16:34.069 --> 00:16:37.748
Air in the outer ear, bone in the middle, fluid in the inner.

287
00:16:37.748 --> 00:16:41.366
The oval window turns bone vibrations into fluid vibrations,

288
00:16:41.366 --> 00:16:44.019
and the round window moves the opposite way.

289
00:16:44.019 --> 00:16:46.672
The semicircular canals sit at right angles.

290
00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:50.471
The vestibular nerve is balance; the cochlear nerve is hearing.

291
00:16:50.921 --> 00:16:55.156
Perilymph, in the scala vestibuli and tympani, is low in potassium.

292
00:16:55.156 --> 00:16:57.621
Endolymph, in the scala media, is high.

293
00:16:57.621 --> 00:17:02.234
Potassium and calcium flow into the hair cell, and it releases glutamate.

294
00:17:02.234 --> 00:17:05.331
Low pitch is at the apex, high pitch at the base.

295
00:17:05.331 --> 00:17:10.893
Sound is mechanical and can't cross a vacuum; light is electromagnetic, made of photons.

296
00:17:10.893 --> 00:17:15.760
Light's amplitude is its intensity, in lux; sound's is loudness, in decibels.

297
00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:19.236
Red light has the lowest frequency, violet the highest.

298
00:17:19.236 --> 00:17:23.597
Refraction bends a wave as part of it slows, like an object in water.

299
00:17:23.597 --> 00:17:27.642
Taste and smell are chemoreception, balance is mechanoreception,

300
00:17:27.642 --> 00:17:29.854
and taste is eighty per cent smell.

301
00:17:31.104 --> 00:17:32.790
<v Examiner>The male reproductive system.

302
00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.354
The scrotum keeps the testes three degrees cooler,

303
00:17:36.354 --> 00:17:40.839
with the dartos wrinkling the skin and the cremaster lifting the testes.

304
00:17:40.839 --> 00:17:46.259
The testes make sperm in the seminiferous tubules and testosterone in the Leydig cells,

305
00:17:46.259 --> 00:17:48.751
and the Sertoli cells nourish the sperm.

306
00:17:48.751 --> 00:17:51.803
The epididymis stores the sperm and matures them.

307
00:17:51.803 --> 00:17:54.606
The vas deferens carries them to the ampulla.

308
00:17:54.606 --> 00:17:58.531
The seminal vesicles add sixty per cent of the semen, alkaline,

309
00:17:58.531 --> 00:18:02.580
with fructose and prostaglandins, and form the ejaculatory ducts.

310
00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:08.186
The prostate, round the urethra under the bladder, adds a third, with citric acid and PSA.

311
00:18:08.186 --> 00:18:10.927
The bulbourethral glands clear acidic urine.

312
00:18:10.927 --> 00:18:14.104
The urethra carries urine and semen, and the penis,

313
00:18:14.104 --> 00:18:18.402
with its corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum, delivers the sperm.

314
00:18:19.652 --> 00:18:21.764
<v Examiner>Semen, erection and ejaculation.

315
00:18:22.214 --> 00:18:26.784
Semen is sixty per cent from the seminal vesicles, thirty from the prostate,

316
00:18:26.784 --> 00:18:28.949
and ten from the sperm and the rest.

317
00:18:28.949 --> 00:18:33.218
It carries, feeds, protects and activates the sperm, and it's alkaline.

318
00:18:33.218 --> 00:18:38.569
Two to five millilitres holds fifty to a hundred and thirty million sperm per millilitre,

319
00:18:38.569 --> 00:18:42.297
and a count below about twenty million is linked to sterility.

320
00:18:42.297 --> 00:18:46.205
Erection is parasympathetic: nitric oxide dilates the arterioles,

321
00:18:46.205 --> 00:18:49.813
the corpora cavernosa fill, and the veins are squashed shut.

322
00:18:49.813 --> 00:18:54.443
Ejaculation is sympathetic: the ducts contract, the bladder sphincter closes,

323
00:18:54.443 --> 00:18:57.088
and the bulbospongiosus pumps the semen out.

324
00:18:57.088 --> 00:19:00.636
Erectile dysfunction is being unable to attain an erection.

325
00:19:01.886 --> 00:19:03.208
<v Examiner>Making sperm and eggs.

326
00:19:03.658 --> 00:19:08.915
Spermatogenesis happens in the seminiferous tubules, continuously from puberty.

327
00:19:08.915 --> 00:19:12.841
Spermatogonia divide by mitosis into primary spermatocytes,

328
00:19:12.841 --> 00:19:17.233
then meiosis one and two give spermatids, which become four sperm.

329
00:19:17.233 --> 00:19:22.890
Oogenesis starts in fetal life: primary oocytes arrest in prophase one until puberty.

330
00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:27.148
From puberty, one a month completes meiosis one and is ovulated,

331
00:19:27.148 --> 00:19:31.474
and meiosis two finishes only if a sperm enters, giving one ovum.

332
00:19:31.474 --> 00:19:35.466
Both are driven by FSH from the anterior pituitary, with LH.

333
00:19:36.716 --> 00:19:38.508
<v Examiner>The female reproductive system.

334
00:19:38.958 --> 00:19:43.133
The ovaries hold the follicles in their cortex, make the oocytes,

335
00:19:43.133 --> 00:19:45.638
and secrete oestrogen and progesterone.

336
00:19:45.638 --> 00:19:49.748
The fimbriae of the fallopian tubes draw in the ovulated oocyte;

337
00:19:49.748 --> 00:19:52.188
the tube is the site of fertilisation,

338
00:19:52.188 --> 00:19:55.849
and cilia and peristalsis carry the oocyte to the uterus.

339
00:19:55.849 --> 00:20:01.565
The uterus, with its fundus, body, isthmus and cervix, receives and nourishes the embryo.

340
00:20:01.565 --> 00:20:06.831
Its wall is the perimetrium, the myometrium of smooth muscle, and the endometrium,

341
00:20:06.831 --> 00:20:10.235
whose functionalis is shed and whose basalis regrows.

342
00:20:10.235 --> 00:20:12.483
The cervix's mucus blocks bacteria.

343
00:20:12.483 --> 00:20:17.107
The vagina is the birth canal and the copulation organ, and it's acidic.

344
00:20:17.107 --> 00:20:21.924
The vulva is the mons pubis, the labia majora and minora, and the clitoris.

345
00:20:23.174 --> 00:20:25.179
<v Examiner>The ovarian and uterine cycles.

346
00:20:25.629 --> 00:20:26.782
The ovarian cycle.

347
00:20:26.782 --> 00:20:31.648
In the follicular phase, FSH makes a follicle grow into a Graafian follicle,

348
00:20:31.648 --> 00:20:33.249
which secretes oestrogen.

349
00:20:33.249 --> 00:20:35.426
At ovulation, around day fourteen,

350
00:20:35.426 --> 00:20:39.140
an LH surge ruptures it and releases the secondary oocyte.

351
00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:42.982
In the luteal phase the corpus luteum secretes progesterone,

352
00:20:42.982 --> 00:20:46.504
and without a pregnancy it becomes the corpus albicans.

353
00:20:46.504 --> 00:20:47.657
The uterine cycle.

354
00:20:47.657 --> 00:20:52.780
In the menstrual phase, progesterone falls and the stratum functionalis is shed.

355
00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.982
In the proliferative phase, oestrogen rebuilds it.

356
00:20:55.982 --> 00:21:00.144
In the secretory phase, progesterone readies it for implantation.

357
00:21:00.144 --> 00:21:04.754
With no implantation, progesterone falls, and menstruation starts again.

358
00:21:06.004 --> 00:21:07.775
<v Examiner>The male versus female table.

359
00:21:08.225 --> 00:21:13.539
LH: in females it triggers ovulation; in males the Leydig cells make testosterone.

360
00:21:13.539 --> 00:21:17.946
FSH: follicle development, or spermatogenesis via the Sertoli cells.

361
00:21:17.946 --> 00:21:23.065
Timing: females start in fetal life and release eggs from puberty to menopause;

362
00:21:23.065 --> 00:21:25.139
males go from puberty, for life.

363
00:21:25.139 --> 00:21:28.897
Size: the egg is the largest cell, the sperm the smallest.

364
00:21:28.897 --> 00:21:32.656
Meiosis: in females it stops and starts and gives one egg;

365
00:21:32.656 --> 00:21:35.637
in males it's continuous and gives four sperm.

366
00:21:35.637 --> 00:21:39.201
Mobility: the egg is carried by cilia; the sperm swims.

367
00:21:40.451 --> 00:21:42.371
<v Examiner>Male hormones and testosterone.

368
00:21:42.821 --> 00:21:48.421
The hypothalamus releases GnRH, so the anterior pituitary releases FSH and LH.

369
00:21:48.421 --> 00:21:54.452
LH stimulates the Leydig cells to make testosterone, which enhances spermatogenesis.

370
00:21:54.452 --> 00:21:58.401
FSH acts on the Sertoli cells, which nourish the sperm.

371
00:21:58.401 --> 00:22:01.991
Testosterone feeds back to switch off GnRH and LH,

372
00:22:01.991 --> 00:22:05.725
and inhibin from the Sertoli cells switches off FSH.

373
00:22:05.725 --> 00:22:10.966
At puberty, testosterone grows the reproductive organs, and brings pubic,

374
00:22:10.966 --> 00:22:17.284
axillary and facial hair, a deeper voice, more bone density and muscle mass, and libido.

375
00:22:17.284 --> 00:22:23.674
Females make testosterone too, in the adrenal glands, and it drives libido in both sexes.

376
00:22:24.924 --> 00:22:27.186
<v Examiner>Oestrogen, progesterone and the pill.

377
00:22:27.636 --> 00:22:30.932
Oestrogen rises at puberty and drives follicle growth.

378
00:22:30.932 --> 00:22:35.267
It grows the uterine tubes, uterus and vagina; it brings breast growth,

379
00:22:35.267 --> 00:22:40.273
fat on the hips and breasts, a wider, lighter pelvis, and axillary and pubic hair;

380
00:22:40.273 --> 00:22:43.875
and it rebuilds the endometrium in the proliferative phase.

381
00:22:43.875 --> 00:22:48.392
Progesterone, from the corpus luteum, readies the lining for implantation,

382
00:22:48.392 --> 00:22:51.445
and in pregnancy develops the lobules and alveoli.

383
00:22:51.445 --> 00:22:56.939
The pill, synthetic oestrogen and progestin, stops ovulation, thickens the cervical mucus,

384
00:22:56.939 --> 00:22:58.526
and thins the endometrium.

385
00:22:59.776 --> 00:23:02.336
<v Examiner>The female sexual response, and four words.

386
00:23:02.786 --> 00:23:05.672
The female sexual response is like the male's:

387
00:23:05.672 --> 00:23:10.941
the clitoris and vaginal mucosa engorge with blood, the vestibular glands lubricate,

388
00:23:10.941 --> 00:23:15.457
and orgasm brings muscle tension, a faster pulse, higher blood pressure,

389
00:23:15.457 --> 00:23:17.966
and rhythmic contractions of the uterus.

390
00:23:17.966 --> 00:23:19.785
Menarche is the first period.

391
00:23:19.785 --> 00:23:24.301
Menopause is when the reproductive hormones decline and the cycles stop.

392
00:23:24.301 --> 00:23:25.932
Amenorrhoea is no periods.

393
00:23:25.932 --> 00:23:28.002
Dysmenorrhoea is painful periods.

394
00:23:29.252 --> 00:23:31.642
<v Examiner>Sperm, eggs and ovulation, fine print.

395
00:23:32.092 --> 00:23:37.563
The sperm head holds twenty-three chromosomes as chromatin, capped by the acrosome,

396
00:23:37.563 --> 00:23:39.672
whose enzymes penetrate the egg.

397
00:23:39.672 --> 00:23:45.538
The midpiece mitochondria turn fructose into A T P for swimming, and the tail propels it.

398
00:23:45.538 --> 00:23:48.043
Sperm don't carry food in their heads.

399
00:23:48.043 --> 00:23:53.316
A diploid spermatogonium divides by mitosis into a diploid primary spermatocyte,

400
00:23:53.316 --> 00:23:57.667
then meiosis gives haploid secondary spermatocytes and spermatids,

401
00:23:57.667 --> 00:24:00.106
and spermiogenesis makes spermatozoa.

402
00:24:00.106 --> 00:24:03.270
Inhibin slows production when the count is high.

403
00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:06.170
Oogonia divide by mitosis only before birth.

404
00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:10.257
Meiosis one gives a haploid secondary oocyte and a polar body,

405
00:24:10.257 --> 00:24:13.091
and meiosis two completes at fertilisation.

406
00:24:13.091 --> 00:24:14.673
Polar bodies degenerate.

407
00:24:15.123 --> 00:24:20.261
Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty to death, with equal cytoplasm,

408
00:24:20.261 --> 00:24:22.205
and gives four motile sperm.

409
00:24:22.205 --> 00:24:28.037
Oogenesis is monthly, menarche to menopause, unequal, and gives one non-motile ovum.

410
00:24:28.037 --> 00:24:30.329
It's mitosis first, then meiosis.

411
00:24:30.329 --> 00:24:35.120
At ovulation, the LH peak bursts the Graafian, or tertiary, follicle,

412
00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:38.661
and the secondary oocyte is pushed to the fimbriae.

413
00:24:38.661 --> 00:24:41.577
FSH is highest in the proliferative phase.

414
00:24:41.577 --> 00:24:45.465
Oestrogen peaks just before ovulation and stimulates LH.

415
00:24:45.465 --> 00:24:48.451
Progesterone dominates the secretory phase.

416
00:24:48.451 --> 00:24:52.478
Her key: LH stimulates progesterone from the thecal cells.

417
00:24:53.728 --> 00:24:56.096
<v Examiner>Erection, the breast, and her female words.

418
00:24:56.546 --> 00:25:01.567
A parasympathetic trigger, then nitric oxide is released, the central artery,

419
00:25:01.567 --> 00:25:05.220
arterioles and spongy tissue widen, more blood flows in,

420
00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:11.024
the corpora cavernosa fill and squash the veins, and last the blood drains back out: two,

421
00:25:11.024 --> 00:25:12.524
five, four, three, one.

422
00:25:12.524 --> 00:25:16.633
An enlarged prostate, not a testicle, makes it hard to urinate.

423
00:25:16.633 --> 00:25:19.763
Prostaglandins drive peristalsis in both tracts.

424
00:25:19.763 --> 00:25:22.306
Males secrete testosterone, FSH and LH.

425
00:25:22.306 --> 00:25:24.915
The breast sits on the pectoralis major.

426
00:25:24.915 --> 00:25:27.393
The alveoli make milk under prolactin,

427
00:25:27.393 --> 00:25:30.785
and the lactiferous ducts release it under oxytocin.

428
00:25:30.785 --> 00:25:34.502
The areola has sebaceous glands and darkens in pregnancy,

429
00:25:34.502 --> 00:25:37.698
and the nipple hardens under sympathetic stimuli.

430
00:25:38.148 --> 00:25:41.592
Oestrogen gives primary breast development at puberty,

431
00:25:41.592 --> 00:25:44.782
and progesterone further development in pregnancy.

432
00:25:44.782 --> 00:25:48.354
Breast development is a secondary sexual characteristic,

433
00:25:48.354 --> 00:25:51.608
and the breast has no strong muscles supporting it.

434
00:25:51.608 --> 00:25:55.499
Ovulation releases an egg, fertilisation fuses egg and sperm,

435
00:25:55.499 --> 00:26:00.220
menstruation sheds the stratum functionalis, and pregnancy is forty weeks.

436
00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:05.961
Abortion is the termination of a pregnancy by removal or expulsion of an embryo or foetus.

437
00:26:05.961 --> 00:26:10.809
The uterus is hollow and pear-shaped, the ovary makes oestrogen and oocytes,

438
00:26:10.809 --> 00:26:14.700
and the placenta feeds the foetus through the umbilical cord.

439
00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:17.380
Oestrogen enlarges the external genitalia.

440
00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.230
<v Examiner>The closed-question extras.

441
00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:24.713
Down syndrome is trisomy twenty-one, Edwards trisomy eighteen.

442
00:26:24.713 --> 00:26:27.250
Turner: a woman with one X, forty-five.

443
00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:30.048
Klinefelter: a man with X X Y, forty-seven.

444
00:26:30.048 --> 00:26:32.910
The most common fetal test is amniocentesis.

445
00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:36.813
In the dark, the pupils dilate: radial muscles, sympathetic.

446
00:26:36.813 --> 00:26:42.148
In bright light and near vision they constrict: circular muscles, parasympathetic.

447
00:26:42.148 --> 00:26:45.596
The lens is biconvex, the image is real and inverted,

448
00:26:45.596 --> 00:26:48.393
half the fibres cross at the optic chiasma,

449
00:26:48.393 --> 00:26:51.516
and the corpus callosum combines the two images.

450
00:26:51.516 --> 00:26:55.484
The eyelids are the palpebrae, lacrimal secretions are tears,

451
00:26:55.484 --> 00:26:57.501
and pinkeye is the conjunctiva.

452
00:26:57.951 --> 00:27:01.280
Blepharitis is inflamed oil glands in the eyelids.

453
00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:06.208
Retinitis pigmentosa kills the rods first, so night blindness comes first.

454
00:27:06.208 --> 00:27:12.001
Macular degeneration loses central vision, and the dry kind has no effective treatment.

455
00:27:12.001 --> 00:27:15.464
Diabetes causes cataracts, glaucoma and retinopathy.

456
00:27:15.464 --> 00:27:20.524
Glaucoma is treated with beta blockers, conjunctivitis with chloramphenicol,

457
00:27:20.524 --> 00:27:24.852
astigmatism with toric lenses, and retinal detachment with laser.

458
00:27:25.302 --> 00:27:31.047
Ototoxic drugs, like gentamicin, furosemide, cisplatin, high-dose aspirin and quinine,

459
00:27:31.047 --> 00:27:33.718
kill hair cells: sensorineural deafness.

460
00:27:33.718 --> 00:27:37.325
High frequencies are heard at the base of the cochlea,

461
00:27:37.325 --> 00:27:39.930
low ones furthest from the oval window.

462
00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:43.403
Loud sound is felt as vibration by mechanoreceptors.

463
00:27:43.403 --> 00:27:49.281
The Doppler effect is a change in frequency for a moving observer, like a passing siren.

464
00:27:49.731 --> 00:27:52.583
The egg is fertilised in the fallopian tube.

465
00:27:52.583 --> 00:27:57.964
Her key calls the outer layer the zona radiata, and calcium lets the sperm through.

466
00:27:57.964 --> 00:28:03.798
Conception is most likely on days ten to seventeen, and ovulation kits detect the LH peak.

467
00:28:03.798 --> 00:28:08.790
The corpus luteum makes progesterone, oestrogen and relaxin for three months,

468
00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:14.041
then the placenta takes over; without a pregnancy it becomes the corpus albicans.

469
00:28:14.041 --> 00:28:16.050
A pregnancy test detects H C G.

470
00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:18.773
An embryo becomes a foetus at eight weeks.

471
00:28:18.773 --> 00:28:21.236
The mini pill never releases oxytocin.

472
00:28:21.236 --> 00:28:27.006
Breast cancer risks: little breastfeeding, early periods, late menopause, family history,

473
00:28:27.006 --> 00:28:28.238
and men get it too.

474
00:28:29.488 --> 00:28:30.625
That's every answer.

475
00:28:30.625 --> 00:28:31.536
Go and get them.
